Troubleshooting
Use the following open-ended questions to diagnose problems yourself rather than following fixed steps:

If output/dlq/failed/ never receives any files even though input/dlq/ messages were processed, what does that imply about the actual failure rate versus the configured maximumRedeliveries, and how would you use application.log to confirm whether messages are succeeding on retry before exhausting redeliveries?
If the application process will not stop with a plain kill <PID>, what does that tell you about in-flight route processing or the shutdownTimeout setting, and what would you check in the logs before resorting to kill -9?
Conclusion
In this lab you built a single Camel application that demonstrates four complementary error-handling patterns side by side: automatic retry with a default error handler, dead letter channel routing for messages that exhaust their redeliveries, in-route fallback processing using both doTry/doCatch and onException, and exception-type-specific routing that separates validation errors, runtime errors, and general errors into distinct outcomes. Running the load test and analysis scripts against these routes showed concretely how failure rate, redelivery count, and exception type together determine whether a message ends up processed successfully, retried, redirected to a fallback, or parked in a dead letter queue. Retry policies are the right choice for transient, self-resolving failures; dead letter channels are best when failures need human or downstream review without blocking the main flow; fallback processing suits cases where an alternative, degraded result is acceptable; and exception-specific routing is valuable when different failure categories require different business responses. From here, consider extending the lab with a real message broker such as ActiveMQ or Kafka in place of file endpoints, and explore Camel's circuit breaker (Resilience4j) EIP for protecting calls to unreliable external services.
